# hbsetbg

`hbsetbg` is the background-setting utility used by Hackedbox.

It loads an image and applies it to the desktop/root window. It is designed to work with the Hackedbox background system while also supporting direct invocation for testing and development.

Usage:

hbsetbg [option] <path to image>

## Options

-center, --center <image>
    Display the image at its original size, centered on the screen.

## Hackedbox Integration

`hbsetbg` is built alongside Hackedbox and is used by the Hackedbox style and background system.

Users normally do not need to invoke `hbsetbg` directly. Backgrounds are configured through the Hackedbox style configuration, and Hackedbox invokes `hbsetbg` when applying a configured background.

Style properties include:

### `root.background`

Sets a single background image.

Example:

root.background: /path/to/image.jpg

### `root.background.mode`

Controls how the background image is displayed.

The available modes correspond to the background modes supported by `hbsetbg`.

### `root.background.folder`

Specifies a folder containing background images.

It is used together with `root.background.timer` to rotate backgrounds automatically.

Example:

root.background.folder: ~/.hackedbox/backgrounds

### `root.background.timer`

Sets the interval, in minutes, used when rotating backgrounds from `root.background.folder`.

Example:

root.background.timer: 5

`5` = 5 minutes.

## Purpose

`hbsetbg` provides the actual background-setting functionality while keeping background configuration within the Hackedbox style system.

This separates the responsibilities:

Style configuration
        ↓
Hackedbox style system
        ↓
hbsetbg
        ↓
Root window

`hbsetbg` remains a separate executable within the Hackedbox project, while the configuration and management of backgrounds remain part of Hackedbox.

HbTK is an independent toolkit and is not required by `hbsetbg`.

## Status

`hbsetbg` was developed specifically for Hackedbox and provides the background-setting functionality required by its style system.

It removes the need for users to manually manage the root background through external background-setting commands.

Direct invocation is available for development, testing, and debugging, while normal operation is handled automatically by Hackedbox.
